# sdinthrough: build a parser, one failing test at a time

Writing code and getting immediate feedback about its correctness is satisfying, but most LeetCode-style programming challenges grade small snippets of code, so you can't practice writing large programs with them.
At the other end of the spectrum are the long tutorials that teach you a complex concept, but they don't give you immediate feedback; they expect you to self-evaluate whether you are on the right path.

sdinthrough is a long-form coding challenge with 600+ testcases where you build a large program while receiving instant feedback.
Each testcase shows you exactly what your program should be printing next, and your job is to write only the small missing piece that gets you there, a bit like following a LEGO manual, and definitely ``em|not`` like this:

!html <img src=sdinthrough.avif class="cCenter" alt="a silly draw-an-owl meme about implementing sdin">

sdinthrough's interface is a simple binary running on your machine, constantly reevaluating your solution.
Hit save, get verdict.
No cloud, no account, no network once installed.

The challenge is long enough to take weeks to complete, and by the end you'll have built a tokenizer, a parser, and JSON-conversion utilities for @/sdin, a config language I designed for this challenge.
You practice data structure design, recursion, AST manipulation, and, most importantly, refactoring.
Some requirements have surprising depth to them, and once tested for, they can topple your data structures and algorithms; can you foresee the traps you might be coding yourself into?
Each step is small, but the complexity keeps growing, and eventually tech debt will bite you if you tread hastily.

(Can AI easily solve this? Yes, and a forklift can lift your barbell too.
If you struggle to avoid AI, then I recommend ``https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pair_programming|pair programming`` with a friend, both committing to avoid AI.
Parsing code is bug-heavy, so this is a debugging-heavy challenge.
Hunting a hideous bug together with caveman tools is a friendship-building experience.)

Your solution can be in any programming language that reads stdin and writes stdout.
Choose your poison carefully.

You need an editor and a terminal running side by side:

- Here on the left-hand side is the Python skeleton code implementing the ``https://github.com/ypsu/sdinthrough/blob/main/docs/runner.md|protocol`` of the challenge.
- On the right-hand side you have the output of `go run github.com/ypsu/sdinthrough@latest solution.py` running in a terminal.
  It watches solution.py for changes.

```split
import sys

for line in sys.stdin:
    parts = line.rstrip().split(' ', 2)
    if len(parts) < 3: break
    sys.stderr.write('==NextNotes==\n')
    sys.stdout.write('==NextAnswer==\n')
    input = bytes.fromhex(parts[2][5:]).decode()
    sys.stdout.write(input)
